NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows the I2C, SPI and RS232 interpreters in action.

There were five separate bursts of activity on the various interfaces being captured by
the LogicPort. Using sample compression the LogicPort had no trouble capturing more than
780 megasamples of data in its buffer over a period of nearly 8 seconds. Most logic analyzers 
without sample compression would have run out of buffer memory long before this sequence
ended.

The first burst of activity can be seen at the trigger position. A cursor has been positioned near 
each of the remaining bursts. From within the Waveforms or State List tab, you can quickly 
scroll to cursors A through F by pressing the <A> through <F> keys on the keyboard. You can 
also scroll to the trigger position by pressing the <T> key on the keyboard.

Be sure to take a look at the State List tab for this example.The "Unique States" format has 
been selected for the state list, causing inactive periods to be condensed. The state list can be 
particularly helpful when using interpreters.

At the trigger position you can see that the I2C bus controller sets up a device at address 50h 
for reading, then proceeds to read 5 bytes of data from the device. Hover your mouse cursor 
over one of the mnemonics (such as A, S or Sr) to see a more detailed description.

At cursor A, 1.18 seconds later, you can see that the RS232 controller sent the string *RCL1 
along with a line feed.

At cursor B, 1.00 seconds later, you can see that the RS232 controller sent the string *RCL2 
along with a line feed.

At cursor C, 2.86 seconds later, you can see that the SPI controller transferred 7 bytes of data 
to four slave devices. Because the SPI controller used a faster clock then the other controllers, 
you will need to zoom in to see the details of these transfers (when viewing the waveforms tab). 
You can quickly zoom in or out by pressing the <+> or <-> keys on the keypad of your keyboard. 

Notice that multiple instances of each interpreter are possible. In this case, we have used four 
additional instances which "pick out" the data destined for each of the four slave devices. 
Device U1 has an 8 bit shift register, U2 is 16 bits, U3 is 24 bits, and U4 is 8 bits. You can
click on the + or - sign next to the name of each interpreter to show or hide the waveforms
for its constituent signals.

The Synchronous Serial/SPI interpreter is very flexible and can be used for other protocols 
such as 3-Wire, Microwire, and PS/2 keyboard and mouse interfaces. It can also be used
for general purpose serial to parallel conversion.

At cursor D, 105 milliseconds later, you can see that the RS232 controller sent the string *RCL3 
along with a line feed.

Note that the sample buffer was actually less than half full when this acquisition was terminated. 
The acquisition was ended early because it was known that no more data would be transmitted. 
You can end an acquisition early using the "Trigger Immediate" function while the buffer is 
being post-filled.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.
/
